  <data name="Rules_HelpTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>How rules work</value></data>
  <data name="Rules_Help" xml:space="preserve"><value>Write one rule per line. The first rule that matches a connection decides, and a connection that no rule matches is allowed.
A rule starts with + (allow) or - (block), followed by a target and optionally a protocol with ports. Anything after # is a comment.
Targets are * (everything), LAN, Internet, Localhost, an IP address such as 1.1.1.1, or a range such as 10.0.0.0/8 or 2001:db8::/32.
Protocols are TCP, UDP, ICMP, * or a protocol number. Ports are a number such as 443, a range such as 1000-2000, or a name such as HTTPS, and */443 means TCP and UDP.
Incoming rules compare the port on this PC that the connection arrives at.
Examples: "+ LAN" allows the local network, "- * TCP/25" blocks sending mail directly, and "+ 1.1.1.1 TCP/443" followed by "- Internet" allows only that one server on the Internet.
Windows sends ping requests itself, so ICMP rules do not affect ping.</value></data>
