  <data name="Memory_FreeNote" xml:space="preserve"><value>Free is almost always close to zero. Windows fills unused RAM with the contents of files that were read or written recently (standby cache) and gives that RAM to a program the moment it needs it. To keep it small, set a standby limit in Settings.</value></data>
  <data name="SettingsPage_35.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Keep the standby cache small</value></data>
  <data name="SettingsPage_36.Content" xml:space="preserve"><value>Off</value></data>
  <data name="SettingsPage_37.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Windows cannot switch its standby cache off; it refills it with every file that is read or written. With a limit, the History service checks every 10 seconds and empties the cache when it is larger, so the RAM shows as free. Programs and files you use again are then read from disk instead of RAM, which makes their next start slower. Needs the History service; changing it needs administrator approval.</value></data>
  <data name="Standby_On" xml:space="preserve"><value>On: the History service empties the standby cache whenever it grows above {0}.</value></data>
  <data name="Standby_Off" xml:space="preserve"><value>Off: Windows keeps file contents in the standby cache until programs need the RAM.</value></data>
