  <data name="RulesPage_BlockAll" xml:space="preserve"><value>Block all network access</value></data>
  <data name="RulesPage_BlockAllNote" xml:space="preserve"><value>Blocks every connection this app makes and closes its open connections. While this block is on, the switches and rules below do not matter.</value></data>
  <data name="RulesPage_OrderNote" xml:space="preserve"><value>The first matching rule decides. A connection that no rule matches is allowed.</value></data>
  <data name="RulesPage_IncomingNote" xml:space="preserve"><value>These rules apply to connections that other devices make to this app. The port is the port on this PC.</value></data>
  <data name="RulesPage_Memory" xml:space="preserve"><value>Memory</value></data>
  <data name="RulesPage_Limit" xml:space="preserve"><value>Memory limit</value></data>
  <data name="RulesPage_LimitUnit" xml:space="preserve"><value>MB</value></data>
  <data name="RulesPage_LimitNote" xml:space="preserve"><value>Keeps the private memory of all of this app's processes together below the limit, much like memory.high in Linux cgroups. When the app goes over it, the History service takes pages out of the app's RAM, largest processes first. The pages move to the standby cache, and from there to disk when Windows needs the room. The app keeps running and reads the data back when it needs it, so a limit that is too small makes it slow. The limit applies to RAM, not to committed memory.</value></data>
  <data name="RulesPage_LimitTooLow" xml:space="preserve"><value>The memory limit must be at least {0} MB.</value></data>
  <data name="RulesPage_Priority" xml:space="preserve"><value>Memory priority</value></data>
  <data name="RulesPage_PriorityNote" xml:space="preserve"><value>When Windows needs memory, it takes low-priority pages out of the standby cache first. With a low priority for background apps, your important apps keep their data in RAM longer. The priority applies to running and newly started processes.</value></data>
  <data name="RulesPage_NeedsService" xml:space="preserve"><value>The History service enforces memory rules, and it is not running. Turn it on under Settings &gt; History. Network rules work without it.</value></data>
