  <data name="Tuning_Intro" xml:space="preserve"><value>Here you set how Windows handles memory, much like sysctl and cgroups on Linux. Every change needs administrator approval.</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_CacheTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Cache</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_Off" xml:space="preserve"><value>Off</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_Standby" xml:space="preserve"><value>Keep the standby cache small</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_StandbyNote" xml:space="preserve"><value>Windows cannot switch its standby cache off, because it refills it with every file that is read or written. With a limit, the History service checks every 10 seconds and empties the cache when it is larger, so the RAM shows as free. Programs and files you use again are then read from disk instead of RAM, which makes their next start slower.</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_CacheTrim" xml:space="preserve"><value>Remove written files from RAM</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_CacheTrimNote" xml:space="preserve"><value>Windows keeps everything written to disk in its file cache. The History service removes files of 256 MB or more (game updates, installers, downloads) from RAM 30 seconds after they were written, and again after 5 minutes. Files a running program still uses stay in RAM.</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_NoService" xml:space="preserve"><value>The History service is not running. Turn it on under Settings &gt; History so this setting takes effect.</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_ManagerTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Windows memory manager</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_StateUnknown" xml:space="preserve"><value>The current state is unknown, because only an administrator can read it. Run the app as administrator, or turn on the History service, which records it every 10 minutes.</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_StateAsOf" xml:space="preserve"><value>As of {0}. The SysMain service is {1}.</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_SysMainRunning" xml:space="preserve"><value>running</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_SysMainStopped" xml:space="preserve"><value>not running</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_Compression" xml:space="preserve"><value>Memory compression</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_CompressionNote" xml:space="preserve"><value>Windows compresses rarely used pages in RAM instead of writing them to disk. This saves RAM and costs some CPU time. The “Memory Compression” process shows how much is compressed. When it is off, Windows writes more to disk.</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_PageCombining" xml:space="preserve"><value>Page combining</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_PageCombiningNote" xml:space="preserve"><value>Windows looks for identical memory pages in different programs and keeps only one copy. This saves some RAM and costs CPU time now and then.</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_Prefetch" xml:space="preserve"><value>Application launch prefetching</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_PrefetchNote" xml:space="preserve"><value>Windows remembers which files a program reads when it starts and loads them ahead of the next start. Programs start faster, and the standby cache fills up more.</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_PreLaunch" xml:space="preserve"><value>Application prelaunch</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_PreLaunchNote" xml:space="preserve"><value>Windows starts Store apps you use often in the background ahead of time, so they are ready at once. When it is off, they start only when you open them.</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_SysMain" xml:space="preserve"><value>SysMain (formerly Superfetch)</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_SysMainNote" xml:space="preserve"><value>This Windows service predicts which programs you will use soon and loads their files into the standby cache ahead of time. When it is off, more RAM stays free and programs start a little slower. Application launch prefetching needs this service.</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_RestartNote" xml:space="preserve"><value>Memory compression, page combining and prefetching change completely only after a restart. SysMain stops or starts at once.</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_RulesTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Memory rules per app</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_RulesNote" xml:space="preserve"><value>A memory limit and a memory priority for one app, like a cgroup on Linux. You set them on the Rules page, together with the app's network rules.</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_OpenRules" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open rules</value></data>
  <data name="Tuning_NoRules" xml:space="preserve"><value>There are no memory rules yet.</value></data>
